    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The third design commitment of hash-first is that a hash which is reserved but not yet uploaded is **queryable as `pending`** \u2014 locally through `stat()` and across reactors through `IAttachmentTransport`. Without it, a peer receiving an action that references an in-flight upload could not distinguish a byte stream that is coming from one that will never exist.\n\nThe two local surfaces behave differently on purpose: `stat()` **succeeds**, returning `status: \"pending\"` with the declared `sizeBytes` and `expiresAtUtc`, while `get()` **throws `AttachmentPending`** \u2014 a class deliberately *not* derived from `AttachmentNotFound`, because pending means \"retry later\", not \"unknown\". A UI can therefore render a placeholder with a known size and a known deadline.\n\nAcross reactors the transport returns `pending` with the expiry, and the peer backs off and retries **until that expiry** \u2014 bounded, so there is no infinite polling; after expiry the peer treats the hash as not found.\n\nThe failure table follows from this. An upload abandoned after the action was submitted reads `pending` until the reservation expires and then `AttachmentNotFound`, leaving the already-tolerated dangling ref; the client can retry by re-reserving the same hash, since no attachment row exists. A client that crashes mid-upload loses only the upload \u2014 the action is already durable server-side \u2014 and a pending-upload queue in `reactor-browser` re-drives it on the next visit.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor/docs/attachments-hash-first.md` \u2014 Summary (commitment 3); Pending status; Syncing pending state through the transport; Failure modes\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
